      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Tarrowby Logistics, a fictional firm. A pack is due at a client by Monday and the file share has slowed to a crawl. First you make the calls anyone would under a deadline; then you see, six months on, where each shortcut led. Then you sort a short set of found sticks and shortcut messages, and match each shortcut to the approved way. Only the sort is scored.",
        "flipHeading": "Six months later",
        "noBlameLine": "Shortcuts are well meant, usually taken under a deadline. Being caught by one is no fault of yours; Colin was right that the share was slow. The move is to tell IT so the data gets somewhere safe.",
        "mondayCard": "On Monday: pick the shortcut you reach for most, and learn the approved swap for it. A found or posted stick goes to IT unplugged. Work stays in work places, not your personal email, personal cloud or a free online service. If the official way is slow, tell IT rather than build your own way round, and report what you're unsure about using the route in the box below. If personal data went to a personal account, that route brings in the DPO.",
        "defendIntro": "Now go through the found sticks and 'quicker way' messages and decide what to do with each. A found or posted stick goes to IT unplugged; if the official way is slow, ask IT rather than build a way round it. There's no clock on you here.",
        "defendOnly": "Every shortcut here is the kind a busy person takes with good intentions, and every one has a quiet cost. The ones you were caught out by are shown below, with the approved way.",
        "conceptPanel": {
          "heading": "About that stick",
          "text": "The stick that came in the post was ours: an authorised test, and it only tells us a stick was plugged in. We name nobody. Most people who plug a found stick in are being kind, trying to find the owner, or just curious, so the kind move is to hand it to IT."
        },
        "flipMontageByChoice": {
          "personal_email": {
            "send_personal": "Your choice. Months on, the personal inbox was broken into with a reused password; the pack sat in its sent items, and the client got a convincing fake invoice built from it.",
            "ask_nilesh": "The version where the pack went to a personal inbox instead: it was broken into with a reused password and the pack was copied. Remote access left nothing outside the firm to find."
          },
          "free_transfer": {
            "use_free_site": "Your choice. The free transfer site was breached; old public links, including a video with people in the background, turned up in search results.",
            "use_fileshare": "The version where a free site was used: its old links were still live when it was breached. The firm's link expired after seven days, so there was nothing to find."
          },
          "posted_stick": {
            "plug_in": "Your choice. The same stick reached four other firms; one plugged it in and was down for nine days.",
            "to_it": "The version where the stick was plugged in: it carried malware and took a firm offline for nine days. The client confirmed they had posted nothing; IT checked the stick safely."
          },
          "rota_app": {
            "use_app": "Your choice. The rota stayed editable by the departed temp, then vanished when the free app closed the unused account, the week before a busy run.",
            "ask_it": "The version where the rota stayed in the free app: the departed temp could still edit it, and it was lost when the account was closed. IT moved it to the team space in minutes."
          }
        }
